# Hairline figures: placement and registry

Every Hairline figure of this workspace lives in `figures/`, whatever it was made for.
The folder is for figures only: no site copy, dates, tags or project context.

## Layout

```
figures/
  <name>.js          the figure source, the only .js files at this level
  gallery.json       the registry: { "no": 16, "figure": "<name>", "prompt": "…" }, in number order
  index.html         the gallery page, generated, never edited by hand
  react/<Name>.tsx   the React copy of each figure, generated
  gif/<name>.gif     the light and dark GIFs (<name>-dark.gif), generated
  build/             look.mjs output: hairline-<name>.html and its PNG sheets
  archive/           retired figures, read by nothing
```

- A new figure is written straight to `figures/<name>.js`. Nothing else goes at the top level.
- `name` says what the object is, lowercase and hyphenated (`tape-reels`, `coin-stacks`), never
  the product or project it was drawn for. The file, the `name`, the first word of the header
  comment and the registry entry all use it; the React copy is its PascalCase (`TapeReels.tsx`).
  The page shows the name above the prompt.
- Run the skill's checks from `figures/build/`, so their pages and sheets land there:
  `cd figures/build && node ~/.agents/skills/hairline-create/look.mjs ../<name>.js --answer … --edge …`
- Other exports (embed, iframe, GIF) are written on request to `figures/export/`, never committed as part of the gallery.

## When you create, change, rename or remove a figure, in the same change

1. Update `figures/gallery.json`: one entry per figure, `figure` equal to the figure's `name`,
   `prompt` one short line saying what the figure is, the way you would ask for it
   (no product names, dates or site context). `no` is the figure's number: a new figure goes
   at the end with the highest number plus one. A number stays with its figure through
   renames and edits, and a retired figure's number is never given again.
   ```json
   { "no": 1, "figure": "coin-stacks", "prompt": "A portfolio of coin stacks that rebalances toward the one you pick" }
   ```
2. Regenerate the React copies: `cd figures && node ../scripts/export-react.mjs --out react`.
   A removed or renamed figure's old `react/<Name>.tsx` is deleted.
3. Regenerate the GIFs from the workspace root: `node scripts/export-gifs.mjs` (needs ffmpeg;
   only stale ones are remade). A removed or renamed figure's old GIFs are deleted.
4. Rebuild the page from the workspace root: `node scripts/build-gallery.mjs`.
   It exits 1 and writes nothing if a figure is unregistered, an entry names no figure,
   a figure is listed twice, or a React copy or GIF is missing or older than its source.
   Fix the files; do not bypass the check.

Each figure on the page has its own play button (bottom right of the drawing), shows its number,
name and prompt, and three pills: PNG (drawn in the
browser from the figure as it stands), GIF (linked from `gif/`, in the page's theme) and React
(the figure's `react/<Name>.tsx` in a modal, with Copy and Download).

A figure you retire moves to `figures/archive/`, and its entry, React copy and GIFs go.

Node and Chrome need the sandbox lifted. Before handing over, run
`node scripts/build-gallery.mjs --check` and say it passed.
